{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790973489,"nonce":"y+lb8Mau3uJDYFoB8MV4H7IetOU=","title":"Idle ash is resident at 40-55 MB on Linux against stock HashLink's 4 MB","message":"A program that prints a line and sleeps, on the NUC (Linux x86_64), RSS from\n/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/smaps_rollup two seconds in:\n\n    stock hl 1.x      4.0 MB (anonymous 1.2 MB)\n    ash --mode interp 44.7 MB (anonymous 5.3 MB, the ash executable 37.2 MB)\n    ash --mode jit    58.5 MB (anonymous 10.2 MB, the executable 46.0 MB)\n    ash --mode hybrid 53.3 MB (anonymous 7.8 MB, the executable 43.2 MB)\n\nSo the gap is file-backed pages of the 142 MB executable, not heap. Reading\n/proc/\u003cpid\u003e/pagemap for an idle interp run: 9878 present pages, 32 MB of them\nin .text, in runs that are all at least 16 pages long -- the kernel's 64 KB\nfault-around, so each touched page brings in its neighbours. Owners of the\npresent .text pages: LLVM / C++ about 4.7k pages, Rust crates about 1k.\n\nCause: the statically linked LLVM runs ~650 static constructors (.init_array\n0x1468 bytes) at process start, whatever the mode. They and the code they call\n(cl::opt registration, pass and target registries) are spread across .text.\n\nTried: linking with -z keep-text-section-prefix (crates/ash_cli/build.rs)\ngroups the constructors into one .text.startup (116 present pages instead of\n676), but the code they call stays scattered: interp 44.7 -\u003e 41.5 MB.\n\nDirections:\n- a symbol ordering file (lld --symbol-ordering-file) listing the functions\n  startup runs, generated from the faulting addresses, so they share pages;\n- load LLVM as a shared library only when the LLVM tier or AOT needs it, so\n  interp, jit and Cranelift-only runs never map or initialise it.\n\nThe pagemap script is scratchpad present.py in the session that filed this;\nthe method: run the program, read /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/pagemap for the executable's\nmappings, map present pages to `nm -n` symbols.","files":null}]}